## Judgment 1: F4 remedy — CLOSED. A fourth shape exists. It is out of the class and not blocking.
The remedy is exactly as prescribed:
  - b1 resume is expected literally as "sess-fixture".
  - Every other copied token is rejected if it is empty or starts with "-".
  - The start file must end in "/system-prompt.md".
Mutations (mutate.sh + mutations.txt below; the tree was clean after):
  M18 / M19 / M21 (the round-3 survivors) all exit=1 now (b1 2/1/2, b4 2/5/2).
  M10–M15 and M20 all still exit=1.
The fourth shape. I probed non-dash tokens in both variable slots:
  - M22 resume slot -> "WebFetch", and M23 resume slot -> opts.sella: exit=1, KILLED. b1's literal
    pins claude-code.ts's single resume construction site, so every substitution in that slot
    dies there.
  - The b4 resume id (talk/tick path) is still shape-checked only. It cannot become a flag (the
    leading "-" is rejected). A non-dash token is taken as `-r, --resume [value]`'s value.
    Round 3's offline probe shows print mode then resumes a matching session or exits 1 ("No
    conversation found").
  - A colliding or wrong id resumes a different transcript. It does NOT change permissions: the
    full policy tail is re-passed literally on every call ("permissions aren't saved with a
    session", claude-code.ts:64-66) and is pinned by b1/b4 literal equality.
  - M24 start slot -> "/tmp/system-prompt.md", and M25 start slot -> "x/system-prompt.md":
    exit=0, SURVIVE. This is not F4's class:
    - `--append-system-prompt-file <file>` takes a REQUIRED value. Round 3 proved on 2.1.280 that
      even "--allowedTools=Bash" there is consumed as a filename. No token in that slot can widen
      tools or permissions on the vendor.
    - What survives is the ORIGIN of the appended prompt. That is prompt integrity (a
      predictable path another local writer could pre-seed), not the permission boundary W-044
      owns. The shipped shim uses mkdtemp.
Class census: the only positional reads that feed an expected argv are checkPolicy's argv[4] and
argv[2] (talk-boundary.test.ts:206, :216). There are no other instances. valuesAfterAll and the
.includes("--resume") form picks are unchanged since round 3's census.
  A1 (advisory): assert the start file sits under os.tmpdir()/bisellium-sysprompt-*/, which kills
     M24/M25.
  A2 (advisory): the stub mints session_id with Math.random(). The stub is the test's own, so a
     fixed id (or reading sessions/<sella>.json) would let b4 assert its resume id literally too.
     The header's "genuinely can't be predicted" is true only because the test chooses a random
     stub.

